# Engineering Principles

## Simplicity

Choose the simplest solution that satisfies the requirements.

Avoid abstractions until they clearly remove repeated complexity.

---

## Readability

Code is read more often than it is written.

Prefer explicit, understandable code over clever code.

---

## Consistency

Follow existing project conventions before introducing new ones.

Consistency is usually more valuable than theoretical perfection.

---

## Reuse

Reuse existing components and utilities whenever reasonable.

Avoid duplicate logic.

---

## Dependencies

Do not introduce a dependency unless its long-term value clearly outweighs its maintenance cost.

---

## Performance

Optimize only after identifying a real bottleneck.

Do not sacrifice readability for hypothetical gains.

---

## Accessibility

Accessibility is a feature.

Interactive elements should be usable with keyboard navigation whenever practical.

---

## Scope

Implement only the requested feature.

Avoid adding speculative improvements.

---

## Communication

Before making large architectural changes, explain the reasoning.

Prefer plans before implementation.
